# fix symmetry example: LJ atoms on a diamond lattice with an inversion
# center between atoms (space group P-1)
#
# The diamond structure has inversion centers half way between nearest
# neighbors, e.g. at (1/8,1/8,1/8) of the cubic unit cell.  For a box of
# 2 x 2 x 2 unit cells this is (1/16,1/16,1/16) in fractional coordinates
# of the box, so the inversion maps s to 2*(1/16) - s = -s + 1/8.  No atom
# is on the inversion center, so all 64 atoms form 32 pairs.
#
# The diamond structure is not stable for LJ atoms, which prefer a close
# packed structure, so the atoms rearrange while keeping the symmetry.

units           lj
atom_style      atomic

# nearest neighbor distance at the minimum of the LJ potential: 2^(1/6)
lattice         diamond 0.4593
Lattice spacing in x,y,z = 2.5921762 2.5921762 2.5921762
region          box block 0 2 0 2 0 2
create_box      1 box
Created orthogonal box = (0 0 0) to (5.1843523 5.1843523 5.1843523)
  1 by 2 by 2 MPI processor grid
create_atoms    1 box
Created 64 atoms
  using lattice units in orthogonal box = (0 0 0) to (5.1843523 5.1843523 5.1843523)
  create_atoms CPU = 0.001 seconds
mass            1 1.0

pair_style      lj/cut 2.5
pair_coeff      * * 1.0 1.0
pair_modify     shift yes

fix             1 all nve
fix             sym all symmetry p-1_shifted.json

velocity        all create 1.2 87287 loop geom
run             0 post no

